A||||7||ChadMan||Woodworking tips? (real wood dashes)||||1||
Z||000000||ChadMan||05-12-2000||09:08 AM||||Ever since I sat in a 2000 Jaguar XK8, I've been thinking about buying some walnut or mahogany and making my own real wood dash bezels.  But how does one get the super-smooth, shiny look that the wood in Jags, Lexus, Benzes, etc. has?<P>The few wookworking projects I've done so far (two CD racks and a lap desk), I've sanded, stained, and varnished and gotten a finish that feels smooth to the hand, but the texture of the wood is still plainly visible (not just the grain.)<P>There are so many handy people on this Forum, I know somebody knows the answer.  Thanks in advance!||||reg||1
Z||000001||Monkeyman||05-12-2000||09:37 AM||||Tung oil, polish, and lots and lots of labor.  It can be done, but I'll bet you'll spend upwards of 100 hours just polishing the wood.  You MUST start out with a quality wood.  Hardwoods are obviously better.  Mohagany (I didn't spell that right, did I?) is good as is cherry, but you'll get into some serious bucks.  Oak is a nice wood, but I don't know if you can get any that can be sliced thin enough and still look good.||||reg||1
Z||000002||bHooper||05-12-2000||09:44 AM||||Those wood dash pieces are <B>very</B> expensive.  They use the nicest laminates that you can get and polish the heck out of them, like MM said!<P>Haveing removed a few of them in my day, they use a very nice quality piece of wood, to start, and then laminate it with the desired surface.  After that, polish, polish, polish, polish.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fieroshop||05-12-2000||09:53 AM||||I will be posting sometime today the results in making the wood panels. I used real black walnut veneer and after what looks like fifty coats of varnish they look great.<BR>I also have plenty of veneer for sale for those that are interested in making their own panels. I will also tell you how I did it.  <P><p>[This message has been edited by fieroshop (edited 05-12-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||LAMBO||05-12-2000||11:22 PM||||Chadman, I don't think it would be wise to use hardwood in an interior of a car. Hardwood contracts and expands with variations in heat/cold (humidity/dryness) and I can't think of many other environments that have greater extremes than the interior of a car. Those luxo car interiors are using wood veneers over manmade materials like MDF (medium density fiberboard) that resist movement. <P>Over time, those hardwood pieces would start cracking and splitting apart from the extreme conditions. That slick, shiny surface is simply hand polished laquer. By building up several coats and handrubbing in between coats, you eventually create a "thickness" to the laquer that makes the wood veneer seem like its got a coat of wet glass over it. Kinda like a car finish. <P>If you want to learn more about how to use veneer in the application that you asked about, check out my companies website at <A HREF="http://www.woodsmith.com." TARGET=_blank>www.woodsmith.com.</A>  We have serveral articles that pertain to this subject. Good Luck!||||reg||10
Z||000005||RFMike||05-13-2000||09:28 AM||||Chadman,<BR>In this instance, I disagree with the statement regarding solid hardwood.  In general, what Lambo says is true, but I think you could use solids if you do what I describe.<BR>Find a hardwood supplier and hand pick the pieces you need.  Find a large supplier that sells to cabinet shops, and sweet talk them into selling directly to you.  These distributors will have walnut and cherry, and can plane it down to the thickness you need.  Some of them will also have less common varieties, such as arctic birch, (one of my favorites- the harsh climate causes great grain patterns- birdseyes, whorls, etc.).<BR>You can buy whatever species you want that will work with the color of your interior.  If you find a wood that you wonder about, rub a little spit on it- this will be fairly close to the finish.<BR>Buy it planed to or sanded to 3/16" thickness. Buy the piece(s)oversized, because planing usually isn't perfect at the edges. If you can't get the seller to do this, and you don't have a planer, take the pieces to a cabinet shop and have them do it.  Ask them to make the final two passes at a zero setting.  This will reduce chatter marks/ sanding lines.  <BR>Sand it to the final finish with whatever method you prefer.  This step can't be shorted.  You have to do it right, starting with 80 grit, then 120, finishing with 200.  If you are using a finishing sander for this part, finish up with hand sanding.  This will only take about one to two hours, depending on the species and the shop planing/ sanding job. (really).<BR>Here's the good part.  Try this on a piece of scrap first.  Screw the faceplate to the scrap, and a 1/2" backup stock, using small wood screws, in the plate's existing mounting holes.  The screws have to be below the faceplate surface, so the router doesn't ride up over them.  Using a plunge router (you have one, don't you?  If not buy or borrow) with a 3/16" diameter guide collet and straight bit, cut out the holes using the faceplate as a template.  This collet may be hard to find for your particular router, but it is essential to doing the job right.  If you have it, you can cut these out in thirty minutes, once you're comfortable with the technique, and the results will be perfect.  Without it, it will look homemade, unless you are really, really good.<BR>Rout the edges with the a very small profile.  Remember, the stock is only 3/16, or less.  A simple roundover might be a good choice if you aren't adept at routing.  You still have the backup stock attached, and this will help in routing.<BR>Finishing is actually pretty easy, especially on small pieces like this.  The best way is with a high quality laquer sprayer, that preheats the laquer, and dries almost as it hits the surface.  That's what I use.  However, you can still get just as good a finish.  <BR>Buy two cans of Deft clear wood finish.  This is wood finishing laquer in a can.  It will cost you about $10 for both.  After you have done the final sanding, lightly spray the first coat.  As you spray, get a completely uniform "wet" look across the whole surface.  Now stop!  The key here is not to spray too much.  Laquer is NOT a high build, thick product, such as polyurethane.  Five coats of sprayed laquer is probably thinner than one brushed coat of poly.<BR>After it dries thoroughly (about 90 minutes) sand in the direction of grain with 600 grit wet sand paper.  Wipe it down to a totally dust free condition, and spray again.  Repeat  to four or five coats.  If you get a run, or  underspray, don't worry.  Just sand it off.  There are only two things that matter here.  One- no dust in between coats.  If you get dust bumps, sand them off completely.  Two- the final coat has to be nearly perfect.  I say nearly, because you will wet sand the final coat with 1000 grit, and polish with #000000 steel wool.  This will give a handrubbed look, and remove any imperfections.  When you are done, you will have a deep, handrubbed finish.<BR>To avoid splitting/ shrinkage/ spray the back also.  This will stabilize the wood.  It will absorb some moisture, but it will be very slow, and I believe will not be a problem.<BR>When you attach in place, overdrill the holes slightly, and do NOT tighten the screws down all the way.  Locktite them in, loose on the frame (just in contact), so the wood can move.  If you lock it down, you may get cracks.  Your total cost (depending on the wood), if you don't have to buy tools, will be about $40.  This would be a 3-4 hour project for me, not including laquer drying time, once I had the material.<BR>If you need to develop a comfort level, it will take more.<P>Good luck.  I have had some experience in working with wood, and finishing.  If you have any questions, feel free to ask. <BR>Mike ||||reg||1
Z||000006||ChadMan||05-13-2000||10:11 AM||||Wow, thanks, you guys!  I have the knowledge...Now all I have to do is buy a GT with power goodies to put my wood dash in!||||reg||1
Z||000007||Fret Naught||05-14-2000||01:14 AM||||Well Chadman, <BR>I build Custom Acoustic Guitars on the side, I can tell you it isn't all that hard. The key is wood preperation. The thing that most people either don't know or neglect is grain filler. Most decorative woods have a some what open grain. So here is how I do my instruments. 1) Sand flat with 220 grit paper. 2) Apply grain filler this sometimes takes 3 coats to get a complete fill. 3) Sand flat again, not too aggresive,Stain 4)sealer 5) finish.<BR>Now, if you seal the wood then fill and stain, the wood and the filler take the stain different and you get some neat contrast. if you fill stain and seal then the wood and filler take the finish a little different. The key is to do some test patches and see what effect you like.<BR>Another thing, stick with like materials. Oil base or water base, don't mix the two. I am not saying you can't but you save yourself a lot of trouble if you stick to like materials. I spray on several coats of clear finish, sand flat (leveling)starting with 400 grit working up to 1200, then I buff on a wheel. I have brushed the finish on, and sanded/polished with Micro Mesh (great stuff) it just takes longer. If you would like to know more feel free to E-mail me. Guitar building is one of my greatest passions.||||reg||1
